    },
    {
      "id": "fj77a9f",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Echo"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ua3g0m1",
      "content": "• Echo is the repetition of sound caused by reflection of sound waves."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9ku2uqf",
      "content": "• It is heard when sound reflects from a distant surface."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "t5reqkd",
      "content": "• The minimum distance required to hear echo is about 17 meters."
    },
    {
      "id": "uzngnbo",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "9. Uses of Ultrasound"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "8aetfgg",
      "content": "• Medical imaging (ultrasound scanning)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "hrr82hp",
      "content": "• Detecting cracks in metals"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2qfwri7",
      "content": "• SONAR used by ships to detect underwater objects"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "wndzlp7",
      "content": "• Cleaning delicate electronic components"
    },
    {
      "id": "su7zn2m",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "10. Noise Pollution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kx8eyn0",
      "content": "• Noise pollution refers to unwanted or harmful sound."
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "The minimum distance required to hear a distinct echo in air at standard room temperature is 17.2 metres.",
        "Sound waves are longitudinal mechanical waves, unlike light waves which are transverse electromagnetic waves.",
        "Speed of sound increases with an increase in humidity and temperature of the medium."
      ],
      "id": "g2vater"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dlk993a",
      "content": "• It can cause hearing problems and stress."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l93gu9h",
      "content": "• Sources include traffic, industrial machines and loudspeakers."
    },
    {
      "id": "2h1scgj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Important Facts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h29w3p7",
      "content": "• Sound cannot travel in vacuum."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5loxa4g",
      "content": "• Human hearing range is 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "eaqq9cv",
      "content": "• Sound waves are longitudinal waves."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "98ksqop",
      "content": "• Speed of sound in air is about 343 m/s."
    }
  ],
  "enriched": true
}